\chapter{Formatting and output sinks}
The \api{printf} package supplies structured formatting and replaceable output.
Despite its name, it uses brace placeholders rather than C variadic format
specifiers. This keeps native wrappers type-safe and identical across hosts.

\begin{mullcode}
val P <- G:LoadLib("printf") or G:DieWith("$E?");
G:Assert(P:Format("{} + {} = {}", 2, 3, 5) == "2 + 3 = 5");
G:Assert(P:Format("{{{}}}", "x") == "{x}");
var log <- "";
P:SetMainSink(fn(text){ log <- log + text; });
P:Printf("item={}\n", 7);
P:Print("a", "b");
P:Println("c");
G:Assert(log == "item=7\nabc\n");
P:SetMainSink(G:GetStdout());
\end{mullcode}

\api{Format(template, ...)} returns a string. Each \api{{}} consumes one
argument and inserts its display text. Doubled braces produce literal braces.
Malformed braces and mismatched argument counts raise errors.

\api{Printf(template, ...)} formats and writes once to the current sink.
\api{Print(...)} concatenates display strings without separators or a newline.
\api{Println(...)} does the same and appends a newline. All three output
operations return nil.

\api{SetMainSink(callable)} replaces the package's sink. It must accept one
string; its return value is ignored. The initial sink is the runtime's stdout
sink. Because package imports are cached, two bindings obtained by importing
\api{printf} share the same sink configuration. Separate runtime instances
have independent sink state.
